L6 = Lesson("1.6", "Stress and Accent Marks",
    "I can predict which syllable is stressed and explain when a written accent is needed.", "ACTFL Novice Mid · Presentational",
    [("sílaba", "syllable"), ("acento escrito / tilde", "written accent mark (´)")],
    "In English, how do you know whether to say PRE-sent or pre-SENT? Spanish has rules that tell you!",
    [Figure(lambda t: table(t, [["Rule", "Where the stress goes", "Examples"], ["Word ends in a vowel, n, or s", "next-to-last syllable", "ca-sa, ha-blan, ami-gos"], ["Word ends in any other consonant", "last syllable", "es-pa-ñol, ha-blar, re-loj"],
                                ["Word breaks the rules", "a written accent marks the stress", "ca-fé, lá-piz, ma-te-má-ti-cas"]], [2.0 * inch, 1.8 * inch, 2.0 * inch], width=5.8 * inch, font_size=9.5)),
     Text("Accents also tell apart words that are spelled the same: <b>sí</b> (yes) / <b>si</b> (if); <b>tú</b> (you) / <b>tu</b> (your); <b>él</b> (he) / <b>el</b> (the). Question words always carry accents: ¿<b>qué</b>?, ¿<b>cómo</b>?, ¿<b>dónde</b>?, ¿<b>cuántos</b>?"),
     Example("Predict the stress", "Say: examen, papel, árbol", ["examen ends in n → next-to-last: e-XA-men.", "papel ends in l → last: pa-PEL.", "árbol would be ar-BOL by the rule, so the accent marks Á: ÁR-bol."],
             "If a word follows the rules, no accent; if it breaks them, it needs one."),
     KeyIdea("Vowel, n, s → stress next-to-last; other consonants → stress last; accents mark exceptions and distinguish words.")],
    [Problem("Where's the stress in hablan?", "HA-blan (next-to-last)"), Problem("Why does café have an accent?", "It ends in a vowel but is stressed on the last syllable."), Problem("Meaning of el vs él", "the vs he")],
    [S("Calentamiento", [SA("Pronounce: mañana", "mah-NYAH-nah"), SA("Silent letter", "h")], cols=2),
     S("Practica", [SA(q, a) for q, a in [("Stressed syllable: computadora", "do (com-pu-ta-DO-ra)"), ("Stressed syllable: reloj", "loj (re-LOJ)"), ("Needs an accent? lapiz (stressed LA)", "yes: lápiz"),
                                          ("Needs an accent? escribir", "no"), ("tu or tú: ___ eres mi amigo.", "Tú")]], cols=1),
     S("Escribe", [OR("Explain in your own words why “inglés” has an accent but “español” doesn't.", "inglés ends in s, so the rule predicts stress on IN; the accent marks the actual stress on GLÉS. español ends in l and is stressed on the last syllable, following the rule.", lines=3)])],
    tsp("Students apply stress and accent rules.", "Presentational: pronunciation and spelling.", "Workbook Lesson 1.6",
        "PRE-sent vs pre-SENT.", "Clap syllables; stress the rule-predicted one.", "Rule chart; examples.", "Practica.",
        ["Adding accents to every stressed syllable."], ["Rule flowchart card."], ["Find 10 accented words in a Spanish newspaper headline online and explain each."], minutes=25),
    summary="Stress rules tell you when accents are needed.")

